13194139533620 true] [17592186059926 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the right, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,3),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x-2)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,3).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+2,3)=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit upward and get the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3+1)=(4,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x-2)+1$ is $(4,4).$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186060098 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533620 true] [17592186060098 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=-2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,4),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,2\\cdot 4)=(-2,8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-1\\cdot 8)=(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)$ is $(-2,-8).$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186060179 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533620 true] [17592186060179 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-1)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of \n$y=f(x-1).$  After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1+1,4)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally,  we shift the result vertically by $1$  unit up and obtain the graph of\n$y=f(x-1)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,4+1)=(2,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(1)=4.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x-1)+1.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2-1)+1 \\\\  & =f(1)+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x-1)+1.$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242335 1017 #edb/ref 17592186242336 13194139533620 true] [17592186242335 1089 "q-121383" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242335 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533620 true] [17592186242335 1100 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={2}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3?$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242335 1113 #uuid "a10acc39-43de-5cce-97e4-fe2c174ea53c" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242335 1138 false 13194139533620 true] [17592186242335 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={2}^{x-2}+3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={2}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b+3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={2}^{x}$, we have that ${2}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$ Substituting $x=a+2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={2}^{a+2-2}+3 \\\\  & ={2}^{a}+3 \\\\  & =b+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+2,b+3)$ must lie on $y={2}^{x-2}+3.$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242337 13194139533620 true] [17592186242336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242338 13194139533620 true] [17592186242336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242339 13194139533620 true] [17592186242336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242340 13194139533620 true] [17592186242336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242341 13194139533620 true] [17592186242336 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533620 true] [17592186242336 1041 "selection" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242336 1104 #edb/ref 17592186242338 13194139533620 true] [17592186242336 1110 #uuid "c3efcfaf-b884-53eb-acac-188ddab361b3" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242337 1034 "(a-2,3b)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242337 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242337 1111 #uuid "9fa1dedf-3384-504c-b5fe-b7c935c4e2f3" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242338 1034 "(a+2,b+3)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242338 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242338 1111 #uuid "9a2b9367-f612-5594-9d46-96eb84706463" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242339 1034 "(a-2,b-3)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242339 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 1319413