point corresponding to $(-3,4)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3+1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-(4))=(-2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-2,-4+2)=(-2,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-3,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-3+1)=4\\,⟹\\,f(-2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-2)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-2,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242351 13194139533620 true] [17592186242350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242352 13194139533620 true] [17592186242350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242353 13194139533620 true] [17592186242350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242354 13194139533620 true] [17592186242350 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242355 13194139533620 true] [17592186242350 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533620 true] [17592186242350 1041 "selection" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242350 1104 #edb/ref 17592186242352 13194139533620 true] [17592186242350 1110 #uuid "bd6687de-2238-5f37-9e6f-0763d20353bd" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242351 1034 "(1,-2)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242351 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242351 