ross the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-(2))=(4,-2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(4,-2+3)=(4,1).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(4,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n4f(4)=8\\,⟹\\,f(4)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ Substituting $x=4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(4)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(4,1)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+3.$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242358 13194139533620 true] [17592186242357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242359 13194139533620 true] [17592186242357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242360 13194139533620 true] [17592186242357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242361 13194139533620 true] [17592186242357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242362 13194139533620 true] [17592186242357 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533620 true] [17592186242357 1041 "selection" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242357 1104 #edb/ref 17592186242362 13194139533620 true] [17592186242357 1110 #uuid "cb8f0439-1450-54f7-b298-b79bbb50d05a" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242358 1034 "(4,-1)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242358 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242358 1111 #uuid "2b8d5f9a-e300-55ce-b35a-88a9c86329ac" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242359 1034 "(4,4)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242359 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242359 1111 #uuid "9ff28aad-c29c-515c-afb4-4e1238ca2bd3" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242360 1034 "(4,3)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242360 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242360 1111 #uuid "0eeb552e-db1d-5d57-b573-db3e004a76cc" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242361 1034 "(4,2)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242361 1111 #uuid "22a1fc25-0f47-5da2-88fe-7848ed0cfa4c" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242362 1034 "(4,1)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242362 1111 #uuid "3a080da0-b380-5a19-9121-b2d59105962c" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242363 1017 #edb/ref 17592186242364 13194139533620 true] [17592186242363 1089 "q-121418" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242363 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533620 true] [17592186242363 1100 "The point  $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+5?$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242363 1113 #uuid "670d2002-d2ca-56f3-b34d-cba198a70401" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242363 1138 false 13194139533620 true] [17592186242363 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(2,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+5:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3\\cdot 3)=(2,9).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,9)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(9))=(2,-9).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-9)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-9+5)=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x)$, we have that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}f(2)=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=9.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+5 \\\\  & =-(9)+5 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,-4)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+5.$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242365 13194139533620 true] [17592186242364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242366 13194139533620 true] [17592186242364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242367 13194139533620 true] [17592186242364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242368 13194139533620 true] [17592186242364 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242369 13194139533620 true] [17592186242364 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533620 true] [17592186242364 1041 "selection" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242364 1104 #edb/ref 17592186242367 13194139533620 true] [17592186242364 1110 #uuid "eec2f6d6-894c-565c-8778-6ba3d564b96d" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242365 1034 "(-2,-4)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242365 1111 #uuid "4a3e38f7-870d-5c43-b308-779f761d21ec" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242366 1034 "(2,8)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242366 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242366 1111 #uuid "dcdaa654-e1cb-5608-bd8a-9b8eebb79e82" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242367 1034 "(2,-4)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242367 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242367 1111 #uuid "499b9f48-7c20-5f5d-95f0-7450b1e2ede8" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242368 1034 "(-2,-8)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242368 1111 #uuid "cd83fa09-2618-55b4-abc6-34d8e3d3d4f1" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242369 1034 "(2,-2)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242369 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242369 1111 #uuid "3bc867c5-1527-58a5-9aaf-23cc044c851f" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242370 1034 "(2,8)." 13194139533620 true] [17592186242370 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242370 1111 #uuid "1fbd0137-7466-5492-854b-a7318f6f0341" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242371 1034 "(-2,8)." 13194139533620 true] [17592186242371 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242371 1111 #uuid "3f110463-1eba-550f-af19-ac4156f681f0" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242372 1017 #edb/ref 17592186242373 13194139533620 true] [17592186242372 1089 "q-211288" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242372 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533620 true] [17592186242372 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831127/assets/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)?$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242372 1113 #uuid "0fbc72ae-1559-5655-8419-8ec96a874c40" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242372 1138 false 13194139533620 true] [17592186242372 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $5$ units to the right, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-4),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $5$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2+5,-4)=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(3,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ is $(3,-2).$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242373 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242374 13194139533620 true] [17592186242373 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242375 13194139533620 true] [17592186242373 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242376 13194139533620 true] [17592186242373 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242377 13194139533620 true] [17592186242373 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242378 13194139533620 true] [17592186242373 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533620 true] [17592186242373 1041 "selection" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242373 1104 #edb/ref 17592186242374 13194139533620 true] [17592186242373 1110 #uuid "1f5c6c5c-44d3-5c10-8f9d-791c7d973deb" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242374 1034 "(3,-2)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242374 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242374 1111 #uuid "f53e0abc-1eef-5a15-b22e-fddd77bdf41c" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242375 1034 "(3,-8)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242375 1111 #uuid "a37d56e3-e043-5def-ad13-7aa9ca5c8b14" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242376 1034 "(-1,1)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242376 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242376 1111 #uuid "ab1c7863-f5a1-5b43-b8a5-2b3089fc0aac" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242377 1034 "(-7,-8)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242377 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242377 1111 #uuid "182ddc9d-315c-5e41-b8af-a8c7ad6a8c2a" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242378 1034 "(-7,-2)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242378 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242378 1111 #uuid "32119ca0-32e7-5b86-8475-0881f2ce12b1" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242379 1017 #edb/ref 17592186242380 13194139533620 true] [17592186242379 1089 "q-211303" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242379 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533620 true] [17592186242379 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831127/assets/ff2956aa2885a0abddd614a11e632d3dfd1114391a9d7e27d790746c159e7f79.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1?$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242379 1113 #uuid "9f4525c5-5e4e-5b55-8b15-c9fe5125a6ae" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242379 1138 false 13194139533620 true] [17592186242379 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis,\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local **minimum** of $y=f(x)$ to the local **maximum** of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(4,-1),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-3f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(4,-1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3\\cdot (-1))=(4,-3).\n$$\n\n- Next, we flip the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-3f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(4,-3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,-1\\cdot (-3))=(4,3).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we translate the result by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=-3f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(4,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(4,3-1)=(4,2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=-3f(x)-1$ is $(4,2).$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242380 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242381 13194139533620 true] [17592186242380 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242382 13194139533620 true] [17592186242380 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242383 13194139533620 true] [17592186242380 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242384 13194139533620 true] [1759