620 true] [17592186242386 1145 "Notice that the graph of $y=-4f(3x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-4f(3x).$ So, we need to find the image of $(3,1),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-4f(3x),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(3,1).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph  along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{3}$ and get the graph of $y=f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3,1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $4$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,4\\cdot 1)=(1,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-4f(3x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-1\\cdot 4)=(1,-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-4f(3x)$ is $(1,-4).$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242387 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242388 13194139533620 true] [17592186242387 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242389 13194139533620 true] [17592186242387 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242390 13194139533620 true] [17592186242387 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242391 13194139533620 true] [17592186242387 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242392 13194139533620 true] [17592186242387 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533620 true] [17592186242387 1041 "selection" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242387 1104 #edb/ref 17592186242389 13194139533620 true] [17592186242387 1110 #uuid "b84059e6-392d-5ff7-b216-2452474ee3bf" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242388 1034 "(1,3)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242388 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242388 1111 #uuid "2206ebab-a00d-5166-9813-50c7bbb5a8f7" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242389 1034 "(1,-4)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242389 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242389 1111 #uuid "3a241e67-b5fb-5239-9bed-c11ce05a60f9" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242390 1034 "(3,1)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242390 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242390 1111 #uuid "1b8b085c-adbe-5ddf-aa9e-307d6a3e5152" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242391 1034 "(3,-4)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242391 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242391 1111 #uuid "5fe19725-c4d0-5d75-906f-685d14540edd" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242392 1034 "(0,3)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242392 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242392 1111 #uuid "578ed652-b7a7-50fc-b2a7-aec34ebfe02c" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242393 1017 #edb/ref 17592186242394 13194139533620 true] [17592186242393 1089 "q-26408" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242393 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533620 true] [17592186242393 1100 "The point $(-1,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)+1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242393 1113 #uuid "1a300bbe-a008-544f-8314-4d9f7fffaeff" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242393 1138 false 13194139533620 true] [17592186242393 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)+1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit down and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,3-1)=(-1,2).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(2))=(-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,-2+2)=(-1,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)+1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-1)+1=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=2.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242394 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242395 13194139533620 true] [17592186242394 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242396 13194139533620 true] [17592186242394 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242397 13194139533620 true] [17592186242394 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242398 13194139533620 true] [17592186242394 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242399 13194139533620 true] [17592186242394 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533620 true] [17592186242394 1041 "selection" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242394 1104 #edb/ref 17592186242398 13194139533620 true] [17592186242394 1110 #uuid "a7755b91-5d25-5b7f-baeb-83497373fe06" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242395 1034 "(-1,-3)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242395 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242395 1111 #uuid "c99c320f-2a35-5a04-ad64-030678d28fec" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242396 1034 "(-1,3)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242396 1111 #uuid "018bc939-0d73-5794-b54c-fe49e335c40c" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242397 1034 "(-1,1)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242397 1111 #uuid "401014e4-b5bb-53dd-984e-927695931a6c" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242398 1034 "(-1,0)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242398 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242398 1111 #uuid "4f511992-425d-5e2c-9118-8629084264e7" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242399 1034 "(-1,-1)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242399 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242399 1111 #uuid "cbade78c-41b7-5009-a7f2-dea33ed72507" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242400 1017 #edb/ref 17592186242401 13194139533620 true] [17592186242400 1089 "q-26594" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242400 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533620 true] [17592186242400 1100 "The point  $(3,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-1?$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242400 1113 #uuid "d6d26fbc-87de-5a39-ae14-d0903e431edc" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242400 1138 false 13194139533620 true] [17592186242400 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1))=(3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1-1)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(3)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)-1 \\\\  & =-(1)-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-1.$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242402 13194139533620 true] [17592186242401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242403 13194139533620 true] [17592186242401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242404 13194139533620 true] [17592186242401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242405 13194139533620 true] [17592186242401 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242406 13194139533620 true] [17592186242401 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533620 true] [17592186242401 1041 "selection" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242401 1104 #edb/ref 17592186242403 13194139533620 true] [17592186242401 1110 #uuid "71cc3638-abd0-5b43-9039-38f6aabc33fb" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242402 1034 "(3,2)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242402 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242402 1111 #uuid "6fb90d16-e7d0-5e52-a9fd-df72d647e0c2" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242403 1034 "(3,-2)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242403 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242403 1111 #uuid "ad368c5b-0cb2-529b-9e46-903b3993c33c" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242404 1034 "(-3,-3)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242404 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242404 1111 #uuid "6ad6f9c9-32b7-5ae8-beed-184a772b2483" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242405 1034 "(-3,-2)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242405 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242405 1111 #uuid "153c2ce8-7eb9-5641-b916-f32801aad129" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242406 1034 "(3,-3)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242406 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242406 1111 #uuid "2984184b-7a52-589b-81d1-99ec20577215" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242407 1017 #edb/ref 17592186242408 13194139533620 true] [17592186242407 1089 "q-26596" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242407 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533620 true] [17592186242407 1100 "The point  $(3,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242407 1113 #uuid "a08d6bcc-6c8d-59c1-9671-aa0f95bb9926" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242407 1138 false 13194139533620 true] [17592186242407 1145 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(3,2)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,2+2)=(3,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(4))=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-4+2)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-2=2\\,⟹\\,f(3)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242408 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242409 13194139533620 true] [17592186242408 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242410 13194139533620 true] [17592186242408 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242411 13194139533620 true] [17592186242408 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242412 13194139533620 true] [17592186242408 1000 #edb/ref 17592186242413 13194139533620 true] [17592186242408 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533620 true] [17592186242408 1041 "selection" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242408 1104 #edb/ref 17592186242410 13194139533620 true] [17592186242408 1110 #uuid "e82234cd-b2c2-5bfe-9450-e9c9beee279c" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242409 1034 "(3,2)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242409 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242409 1111 #uuid "8bbcd33a-e392-52d2-bc13-c126c751c9f8" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242410 1034 "(3,-2)" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242410 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533620 true] [17592186242410 1111 #uuid "3aedfbff-de2f-5a2b-a4d4-d1c3278fd732" 13194139533620 true] [17592186242411 1034 